# Part Six — Conferences, Reports and Surveys

> The events and documents behind the dates and data you quote. Name the source in your answer — "the **PDHS 2017–18** found…" — and examiners trust the figure.

## 6.1  The UN conferences on women and related summits

::: table | The UN conferences and summits.
@widths 1.6,3.8,8.6
@font 9.5
Year | Conference | Outcome to remember
1975 | First World Conference on Women, **Mexico City** (MCQ 2016) | International Women's Year; UN Decade for Women (1976–85) launched
1980 | Second World Conference, **Copenhagen** | Mid-decade review; CEDAW opened for signature
1985 | Third World Conference, **Nairobi** | Forward-Looking Strategies; DAWN's critique of development
1987 | Safe Motherhood Conference, **Nairobi** | Safe Motherhood Initiative; Fathalla's "Mrs X"
1993 | World Conference on Human Rights, **Vienna** | "Women's rights are human rights"; DEVAW followed
1994 | International Conference on Population and Development, **Cairo** | Reproductive rights and health
1995 | Fourth World Conference, **Beijing** | Beijing Platform: 12 critical areas; 189 states; gender mainstreaming
2000 | Millennium Summit | Millennium Development Goals (MDG 3 on gender equality)
2015 | UN Sustainable Development Summit | SDGs; Goal 5 — gender equality
:::

## 6.2  Global reports and indices

::: table | Global reports and indices.
@widths 1.6,4.6,7.8
@font 9.5
Year | Report or index | Why it matters
1987 | UNICEF, *Adjustment with a Human Face* | Social costs of structural adjustment
1990 | UNDP *Human Development Report* (Mahbub ul Haq) | Human Development Index; development as choices
1995 | UNDP GDI and GEM | First gender indices
2006 | WEF *Global Gender Gap Report* (annual) | Pakistan 112/115 (2006); 143/144 (2017); 148/148 (2025); 143/145 (2026)
2010 | UNDP Gender Inequality Index | Health, empowerment, labour
2012 | World Bank *World Development Report: Gender Equality and Development* | "Smart economics"
— | World Bank *Women, Business and the Law* | Legal equality for women's economic life
2021 | WHO violence estimates | About 1 in 3 women experience physical or sexual violence
2024 | UNODC / UN Women, *Femicides in 2023* | 85,000 killed; 51,100 by partners or family
2025–26 | GSMA *Mobile Gender Gap Report* | Pakistan's mobile-internet gap: 25% (2024) → 8% (2025)
:::

## 6.3  Pakistani reports, surveys and commissions

::: table | Pakistani reports, surveys and commissions.
@widths 1.6,4.6,7.8
@font 9.5
Year | Report, survey or body | Why it matters
1976 | Pakistan Women's Rights Committee | Early review of women's legal status
1983–85 | Zari Sarfaraz Commission on the Status of Women | Report on women's status under Zia
1997 | Commission of Inquiry for Women | Recommended repeal of discriminatory laws
2000 | National Commission on the Status of Women (statutory 2012) | Reviews laws and policies
2002 | National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women | Targets for women's participation
2017–18 | Pakistan Demographic and Health Survey (PDHS) | Fertility 3.6; contraception 34%; violence 28%
2018 | National Nutrition Survey (NNS) | Anaemia 42% of women; stunting 40%
2019 | Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey (PMMS) | MMR 186
2023 | Digital Census | 241 million; female literacy about 53%
2024–25 | Labour Force Survey (PBS) | Female LFPR 24.4%
Annual | HRCP *State of Human Rights* | Honour killings: 405 (2024), 470 (2025)
Annual | SSDO reports on GBV | 32,617 cases; conviction about 0.5% (2024)
Each election | FAFEN election observation | Voter gender gap; turnout by sex
:::
